



/*

Generators in JavaScript
Generators in JavaScript are special functions that allow you to pause and resume execution using the yield keyword.
 They are useful for handling iterators, 
asynchronous operations, and infinite sequences efficiently.




1. Syntax of Generators
A generator function is defined using the function* syntax and uses yield to return values one at a time.

javascript
Copy
Edit
function* generatorFunction() {
  yield 1;
  yield 2;
  yield 3;
}

const gen = generatorFunction();

console.log(gen.next()); // { value: 1, done: false }
console.log(gen.next()); // { value: 2, done: false }
console.log(gen.next()); // { value: 3, done: false }
console.log(gen.next()); // { value: undefined, done: true }


2. Types of Generators in JavaScript
(1) Simple Generator
Basic example that returns values one by one.

javascript
Copy
Edit
function* simpleGenerator() {
  yield "Hello";
  yield "World";
}

const gen1 = simpleGenerator();
console.log(gen1.next().value); // Hello
console.log(gen1.next().value); // World
console.log(gen1.next().done);  // true (No more values)



(2) Infinite Generator
Useful for generating an infinite sequence, such as IDs or numbers.

javascript
Copy
Edit
function* idGenerator() {
  let id = 1;
  while (true) {
    yield id++;
  }
}

const gen2 = idGenerator();
console.log(gen2.next().value); // 1
console.log(gen2.next().value); // 2
console.log(gen2.next().value); // 3



(3) Generator with Parameters
Generators can accept parameters via .next(value).

javascript
Copy
Edit
function* greetGenerator() {
  const name = yield "What is your name?";
  yield `Hello, ${name}!`;
}

const gen3 = greetGenerator();
console.log(gen3.next().value); // What is your name?
console.log(gen3.next("Alice").value); // Hello, Alice!
💡 Use case: Interactive question-answer flows.

(4) Delegated Generators (yield*)
A generator can delegate execution to another generator using yield*.

javascript
Copy
Edit
function* numbers() {
  yield 1;
  yield 2;
}

function* letters() {
  yield "A";
  yield "B";
}

function* combined() {
  yield* numbers();
  yield* letters();
}

const gen4 = combined();
console.log(gen4.next().value); // 1
console.log(gen4.next().value); // 2
console.log(gen4.next().value); // A
console.log(gen4.next().value); // B
💡 Use case: Combining multiple iterators.

(5) Asynchronous Generators (async function*)
Useful for handling asynchronous operations like API calls.

javascript
Copy
Edit
async function* fetchData() {
  yield fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1").then(res => res.json());
}

const asyncGen = fetchData();
asyncGen.next().then(res => console.log(res.value)); // Fetches data
💡 Use case: Streaming API responses.

Key Benefits of Generators
✅ Lazy execution (values generated on demand).
✅ Memory efficient (doesn't store all values at once).
✅ Pause and resume execution (great for async operations).
✅ Better handling of iterators compared to traditional loops.

Would you like a real-world use case of generators? 🚀


*/




